{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Kecebas2017-benchmark-TiO2-SiO2-Ag-MIR",
  "Source": "M. A. Kecebas, M. P. Menguc, A. Kosar, and K. Sendur, \"Passive radiative cooling design with broadband optical thin-film filters,\" J. Quant. Spectrosc. Radiat. Transf. (2017), article in press, pp. 1-8 (volume not printed in this copy). doi:10.1016/j.jqsrt.2017.03.046 - Sec. 2, p. 3 and Fig. 2(a): 'On Si substrate 50 nm thick Ag is coated. Then, 4 layers of TiO2 and SiO2, each 60 nm thick, are added. Finally 3 layers of TiO2 and SiO2, each 300 nm thick'; layer sequence from the Fig. 2(a) scheme",
  "Notes": "Benchmark passive daytime radiative-cooling coating (similar to Raman et al. 2014, ref. [3]): solar-reflecting thin segment on Ag plus thick mid-IR emitting segment - infrared variant: the same layers with measured mid-infrared n, k for sputtered TiO2 and SiO2 (Kischkat et al. 2012), valid 1.54-14.3 um only, to check the 8-13 um emission\nSpecification: High reflectance 280 nm - 4 um (solar), high emissivity (absorptance) 8-13 um.\nReported: Average absorption ~3 % (reflection 97 %) over 280 nm - 4 um; average emission ~45 % in 8-13 um (Fig. 2 caption and p. 3). Fig. 2(b): absorption peak ~1.0 near 9.7-10 um, ~0.15-0.2 at 11-14 um (read off the plot, approximate). Cooling power 35 W/m^2 (p. 3), vs 40.1 +/- 4.1 W/m^2 reported in ref. [3].\nNotes: Same layers as Kecebas2017-benchmark-TiO2-SiO2-Ag. TiO2 and SiO2 are Kischkat et al., Appl. Opt. 51, 6789 (2012) samples TD00-600 and SO40-200 (Brendel fits tabulated 1.54-14.3 um); the paper does not name its own infrared data, so the emission is a comparison, not a reproduction. Ag is Yang et al. 2015, opaque (50 nm on Si in the paper). Emission = absorptance AA at normal incidence. Do not evaluate below 1.54 um.\nLayers from Sec. 2, p. 3 and Fig. 2(a): 'On Si substrate 50 nm thick Ag is coated. Then, 4 layers of TiO2 and SiO2, each 60 nm thick, are added. Finally 3 layers of TiO2 and SiO2, each 300 nm thick'; layer sequence from the Fig. 2(a) scheme; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours mean emission 35.4 % over 8-13 um (Kischkat TiO2 and SiO2, Yang Ag); paper average emission 45 %. The paper does not name its infrared TiO2 and SiO2 data; Kischkat sputtered films are used, so this is a comparison only.",
